<Article slug="oribe-and-shino">
  <p>
    Some of the glazes in my kiln are centuries old. The green I call
    <a href="/surfaces/">Oribe</a> comes from a style of pottery first made in Japan around the end
    of the sixteenth century, and it still behaves much the way it did then: it runs, it pools, and
    it turns darkest wherever it gathers.
  </p>

  <h2>A tea master's taste</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oribe_ware" rel="noopener">Oribe ware</a> was made in
    the Mino region of central Japan. It takes its name from
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Furuta_Oribe" rel="noopener">Furuta Oribe</a>, a warrior
    and tea master whose taste ran to bold, playful and deliberately uneven pots. The best known
    Oribe pieces pair a glossy copper green glaze with bare clay and loose brushed patterns,
    and many were made for use in tea gatherings.
  </p>

  <h2>Why copper turns green</h2>
  <p>
    The colour comes from copper in the glaze. Fired with plenty of oxygen, copper gives the
    familiar olive and sage greens. Where the glaze is thick it reads almost black green; where it
    is thin over an edge it breaks to a lighter, yellower tone. In a soda kiln, the flame and vapour
    add another layer, so the green sits beside rust flashing instead of plain clay.
  </p>

  <h2>Shino, the white sibling</h2>
  <p>
    The same kilns in Mino also produced
    <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shino_ware" rel="noopener">Shino ware</a>, known for a
    thick, soft white glaze that can blush orange or show small pinholes where it is thin. Shino
    style glazes are a favourite of potters who fire with flame and atmosphere for the same reason
    Oribe is: they record the fire instead of hiding it.
  </p>

  <h2>Why old glazes still work</h2>
  <p>
    These glazes were never about perfect, even colour. They reward movement and variation, which
    is exactly what a soda kiln gives you. Look at the
    <a href="/collection/?surface=oribe">pieces in Oribe</a> and you will see the glaze thick in
    the hollows and thin on every facet, the same quality those first Mino potters were after.
  </p>

